    "setup_id": "05601a00c6bc4954635863826740b7b61d18798ad2f99e32a9fef415a652990f",
    "text": "To find the coordinates of point \\(D\\) in parallelogram \\(ABCD\\) with vertices \\(A(0, 1)\\), \\(B(2, 1)\\), and \\(C(4, 0)\\), we can use the property that the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other. This means that the midpoint of diagonal \\(AC\\) is the same as the midpoint of diagonal \\(BD\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate the midpoint of diagonal \\(AC\\). The coordinates of \\(A\\) are \\((0, 1)\\) and the coordinates of \\(C\\) are \\((4, 0)\\). The formula for the midpoint of a segment with endpoints \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is \\(\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\\). Applying this formula to \\(A\\) and \\(C\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Midpoint of } AC = \\left( \\frac{0 + 4}{2}, \\frac{1 + 0}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{4}{2}, \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = \\left( 2, \\frac{1}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSince the diagonals bisect each other, the midpoint of \\(BD\\) must also be \\(\\left( 2, \\frac{1}{2} \\right)\\). Let the coordinates of \\(D\\) be \\((x, y)\\). The coordinates of \\(B\\) are \\((2, 1)\\). Using the midpoint formula for \\(BD\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Midpoint of } BD = \\left( \\frac{2 + x}{2}, \\frac{1 + y}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nWe know this midpoint is \\(\\left( 2, \\frac{1}{2} \\right)\\), so we set up the following equations:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2 + x}{2} = 2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{1 + y}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSolving the first equation for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2 + x}{2} = 2 \\implies 2 + x = 4 \\implies x = 2\n\\]\n\nSolving the second equation for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1 + y}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\implies 1 + y = 1 \\implies y = 0\n\\]\n\nThus, the coordinates of \\(D\\) are \\((2, 0)\\). The sum of the coordinates of point \\(D\\) is:\n\n\\[\n2 + 0 = 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
    "timing_scope": "native prompt render, tokenize, transfer, CUDA-synchronized generate, decode; excludes loading/scoring",
